WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
I clearly broke some of your brains with my relativity series.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.080
You had a lot of really good comments and questions.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.340
Let’s see what you had to say.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.520
Why does a light wave always have to travel at the same speed?

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.380
The speed of any wave is only dependent on the properties of the stuff that’s waving.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.480
For something like water waves or sound,

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.800
that can be things like: material elasticity and mass density.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.400
Both of which ultimately depend on things like temperature.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.880
Very easy to change.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.380
Light is a wave in electric and magnetic fields.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.020
In other words, not matter.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.880
There’s no way to change the properties of those fields, so there’s no way to change the speed of light.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.120
No exceptions.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.020
Which reminds me of something I think some scientists need to learn:

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.120
If your rule has exceptions, it’s not that great of a rule.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.300
Why is “c” the symbol we use for the speed of light?

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.700
Because the speed of light is “constant.”

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.420
Scientists aren’t usually very good at naming or labeling things.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.180
Is there some kind of absolute universal reference frame?

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.020
No, although, they tried really hard to make this a thing back in the day.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.800
Luminiferous aether!

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.100
But it was totally bogus!

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.860
Too many assumptions had to be made and we all know where that leads.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.670
Brian Greene said objects always move through spacetime at the speed of light

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.640
and it's only the direction that we can change.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.140
Well, yes, I even said something like this in a previous video,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.180
but you have to be careful about the definitions of words.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.260
As you can see, the word “object” has many meanings.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.180
As long as you limit the word “object” to things with mass, then you’re fine.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.820
Light itself doesn’t fall under Brian Greene’s statement,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.340
but it doesn’t have mass either, so it isn’t an object.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.680
If you want more details and have a solid math background, my book covers this.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.360
Otherwise, you can wait for my next video.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.420
In the spacetime diagram, how come the second time axis is diagonal or curved?

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.860
If something is stationary, then it’s moving through time and not through space.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.420
In a spacetime diagram, that’s when your time axis lines up with your path.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.920
It has to, by definition.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.120
That’s what it means to be stationary.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.400
If we were on the opposite side of the Earth, would that mean we’re upside down?

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.080
Technically, gravity defines down, so as long as that always points toward your feet,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.100
then you’re right-side up.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.080
What’s a geodesic?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.240
A geodesic is the new name for an inertial
reference frame.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.820
Let’s start all the way back with Newton.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:04.220
His first law defines what an inertial frame is, which is why it’s sometimes called the inertial law.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.220
It says the acceleration is zero in inertial reference frames.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.080
About 230 years later, Einstein says “Actually, no.”

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.700
Acceleration is equal to this.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.320
We call it the geodesic equation and it’s like a new and improved version of Newton’s First Law.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.720
So, when we accelerate, only time is being curved?

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.560
No, whether you’re talking about acceleration or gravity, both space &amp; time are curved.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.580
I left off the new space axis in the diagram for clarity.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.580
But time does tend to be curved more than space,

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.320
so time-curvature is actually where most gravity comes from.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.880
If you’re floating in the middle of empty space, you just sit in one place and only move through time.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.640
Your geodesic is straight and spacetime is flat.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.360
But if we put the Earth in there, your geodesic bends toward the Earth’s surface, so that’s where you go.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.920
It takes something a lot bigger than the Earth to notice curvature in space.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.980
Like a really big galaxy or a whole cluster of galaxies.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:53.800
Or, you know, a black hole or something.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.860
If I drop two coins on opposite sides of the Earth and they each think the Earth is accelerating,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.780
then the Earth is accelerating toward both at the same time.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.300
First of all, each coin is in a different reference frame,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.180
so you can only be looking from the point of view of one at a time.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.780
The Earth doesn’t accelerate toward both,
it accelerates toward each.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.250
Here’s the first coin’s frame, the second
coin’s frame, and the Earth’s frame.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.920
The distinction is very important because curvature is only defined locally.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.640
How are inertial mass and gravitational mass the same?

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.380
It seems like they’re completely independent.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.100
They were seen as independent for a very long time.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.000
In fact, Newton had no idea why they were always the same.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.860
It took General Relativity in 1915 to finally understand.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.620
The equivalence principle says acceleration and gravity are the same thing.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.120
So these two equations are basically saying the same thing.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.360
So the source of gravity isn’t mass?

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.820
Nope. It’s energy.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.100
Well, technically, it’s energy and momentum and pressure and stress.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.580
All summarized in a quantity called the Stress-Energy Tensor.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.740
Meaning anything with energy and momentum can curve spacetime and create gravity, including light.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.940
Light might not have any mass, but it still has energy and momentum.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.160
It’s all kinetic energy, but it’s still energy.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.080
It still has the ability to do stuff.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.720
How can gravity be considered a fundamental force when it isn’t even a force

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.000
and how does the graviton fit into all of this?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.040
This is tricky because the other three fundamental forces aren’t really forces either.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.069
We don’t even call them the fundamental forces.

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:17.240
We call them the fundamental interactions

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:21.059
and understanding them requires a type of physics called quantum field theory.

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:22.830
and gravity doesn’t fit into that very well.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.860
I need to do way more research into QFT before I’m ready to say anything more.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.620
Thanks for commenting. Keep them coming.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.540
Some of the big questions can even inspire whole videos.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.600
Also, thanks for liking, sharing, and subscribing.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.680
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

